\title{A Complexity Theory of Recipes:\\ Cooking, Verifying, and the Kitchen Analogue of P vs NP}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a small, self-contained algebraic theory that takes seriously the analogy
\emph{``every recipe is an algorithm.''} A recipe is modelled by two non-negative integers:
its \emph{cooking time} $C(R)$, the resource required to produce a dish, and its
\emph{verification time} $V(R)$, the resource required to taste the finished dish and judge
whether it is good. This is the culinary shadow of the central distinction of computational
complexity, between \emph{solving} a problem and \emph{verifying} a candidate solution. We
classify recipes into three mutually exclusive classes---\emph{quick} ($C=V$, the analogue
of $P=NP$), \emph{traditional} ($V<C$, the analogue of $P\neq NP$), and \emph{overhard}
($C<V$, the analogue of an $NP$-hard problem whose verification is itself expensive)---and
prove that this trichotomy is exhaustive. We introduce \emph{sequential composition} of
recipes and show it endows the set of recipes with the structure of a commutative monoid,
under which the classes exhibit natural closure properties. We define the \emph{speedup}
$C-V$ and prove it is additive over \emph{physical} recipes ($V\le C$), then use this to
establish the central \textbf{Batch Quickness Theorem}: a menu of physical recipes is
globally quick if and only if every dish on it is quick. We close with a rational-ratio
characterization of the three classes and a discussion of numerical experiments. Every
result stated is fully proved.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The question of whether $P$ equals $NP$ asks, informally, whether every problem whose
solutions can be \emph{checked} efficiently can also be \emph{solved} efficiently. Despite
decades of effort it remains open, and it is widely believed that $P\neq NP$: for a great
many problems, producing a solution is fundamentally harder than verifying one.

This paper explores that distinction in an unexpectedly concrete setting: the kitchen. A
recipe is a procedure that transforms inputs (ingredients) into an output (a dish). Two
resources govern its practice: the effort of \emph{cooking}, producing the dish, and the
effort of \emph{verifying}, tasting the result and deciding whether it meets its standard.
The relationship between these two resources is precisely the relationship at the heart of
complexity theory, and it varies from dish to dish.

Our contribution is a compact, rigorous algebraic model of this relationship, together with
a chain of theorems that mirror---in miniature and with complete proofs---several structural
features one expects of a complexity theory: an exhaustive classification, well-behaved
composition, closure of classes, additive resource accounting, and a global-versus-local
characterization. The model discards everything about a recipe except two integers; this
austerity isolates the single structural invariant that recipes share with computational
problems.

\section{The model}

\begin{definition}[Recipe]
A \emph{recipe} is a pair $R=(C(R),V(R))$ of non-negative integers, where $C(R)$ is the
\emph{cooking time} (the resource to prepare the dish) and $V(R)$ is the \emph{verification
time} (the resource to taste and judge the dish). Two recipes are equal precisely when their
cooking times agree and their verification times agree.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Classes]
Let $R$ be a recipe. $R$ is \emph{quick} if $C(R)=V(R)$; \emph{traditional} if $V(R)<C(R)$;
\emph{overhard} if $C(R)<V(R)$; and \emph{physical} if $V(R)\le C(R)$.
\end{definition}

Quick recipes are the culinary analogue of $P=NP$ (assemble-and-serve dishes such as a
salad). Traditional recipes are the analogue of $P\neq NP$ (the majority of cooked dishes).
Overhard recipes are the analogue of a problem for which even verification is expensive---the
soufflé being the motivating example, since the only reliable check that it has risen
throughout is to cut it open, destroying it. Physical recipes are exactly the quick and
traditional recipes together.

\section{Trichotomy and physicality}

\begin{theorem}[Trichotomy of Recipes]\label{thm:tri}
Every recipe is exactly one of quick, traditional, or overhard: for every recipe $R$
precisely one of $C(R)=V(R)$, $V(R)<C(R)$, $C(R)<V(R)$ holds.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The values $C(R)$ and $V(R)$ are natural numbers, and for any two natural numbers exactly one
of ``equal,'' ``first strictly larger,'' ``second strictly larger'' holds by the totality and
antisymmetry of the order.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Physicality]\label{thm:phys}
A recipe $R$ is physical iff it is not overhard: $V(R)\le C(R)\iff \neg\,(C(R)<V(R))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definition of $\le$ as the negation of the strict reverse inequality on
the integers.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:pqt}
Every physical recipe is quick or traditional; conversely, quick and traditional recipes are
physical.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
If $V(R)\le C(R)$ then $V(R)=C(R)$ (quick) or $V(R)<C(R)$ (traditional). If quick, $C=V$ gives
$V\le C$; if traditional, $V<C$ gives $V\le C$.
\end{proof}

\section{Sequential composition and the monoid structure}

\begin{definition}[Sequential composition]
For recipes $R$ and $S$, their \emph{sequential composition} is
$R\circ S=(C(R)+C(S),\,V(R)+V(S))$. The \emph{empty recipe} is $\mathbf 1=(0,0)$.
\end{definition}

Thus $C(R\circ S)=C(R)+C(S)$ and $V(R\circ S)=V(R)+V(S)$: both resources are additive.

\begin{theorem}[Monoid structure]\label{thm:monoid}
The set of recipes with $\circ$ and identity $\mathbf 1$ is a commutative monoid:
\emph{(1)} $(R\circ S)\circ T=R\circ(S\circ T)$; \emph{(2)} $\mathbf 1\circ R=R=R\circ\mathbf 1$;
\emph{(3)} $R\circ S=S\circ R$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each identity is checked componentwise and reduces to the corresponding property of integer
addition (associativity, neutrality of $0$, commutativity). Two recipes are equal iff both
components agree, so componentwise verification suffices.
\end{proof}

Commutativity encodes a culinary fact: the order in which two independent dishes are prepared
does not change the total cooking and tasting budgets.

\section{Closure of the classes}

\begin{theorem}[Quick closure]\label{thm:qclose}
If $R$ and $S$ are quick, then $R\circ S$ is quick.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
From $C(R)=V(R)$ and $C(S)=V(S)$, $C(R)+C(S)=V(R)+V(S)$, i.e. $C(R\circ S)=V(R\circ S)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Traditional absorbs physical]\label{thm:tclose}
If $R$ is traditional and $S$ is physical, then $R\circ S$ is traditional.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$V(R)<C(R)$ and $V(S)\le C(S)$ add to $V(R)+V(S)<C(R)+C(S)$, i.e. $V(R\circ S)<C(R\circ S)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Physical closure]\label{thm:pclose}
If $R$ and $S$ are physical, then $R\circ S$ is physical.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$V(R)\le C(R)$ and $V(S)\le C(S)$ add to $V(R)+V(S)\le C(R)+C(S)$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:tclose} is the most significant: a genuine slowdown cannot be undone by
composing it with a well-behaved companion. Hardness, once present, is inherited by the whole.

\section{Speedup and its additivity}

\begin{definition}[Speedup]
The \emph{speedup} of a recipe is $\sodom(R)=C(R)-V(R)$, using truncated subtraction on the
non-negative integers (so the value is $0$ when $V(R)>C(R)$).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Speedup characterization]\label{thm:spchar}
A recipe $R$ is quick iff it is physical and $\sodom(R)=0$; that is, iff $V(R)\le C(R)$ and
$C(R)-V(R)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If quick, $C=V$ gives $V\le C$ and $C-V=0$. Conversely, if physical then $V\le C$, so
truncated subtraction is genuine subtraction; $C-V=0$ then forces $C=V$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Additivity of speedup over physical recipes]\label{thm:spadd}
If $R$ and $S$ are physical, then $\sodom(R\circ S)=\sodom(R)+\sodom(S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:pclose}, $R\circ S$ is physical, so all subtractions are non-truncating:
\[
\sodom(R\circ S)=\big(C(R)+C(S)\big)-\big(V(R)+V(S)\big)
=\big(C(R)-V(R)\big)+\big(C(S)-V(S)\big)=\sodom(R)+\sodom(S).\qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

The physicality hypothesis is essential: over the non-negative integers subtraction is
truncated at zero, so without $V\le C$ the two sides may differ.

\section{Repetition: cooking many servings}

\begin{definition}[Repetition]
For $n\in\mathbb N$, define $R^{\circ 0}=\mathbf 1$ and $R^{\circ(n+1)}=R\circ R^{\circ n}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Linear scaling]\label{thm:scale}
For all $n$ and $R$, $C(R^{\circ n})=n\,C(R)$ and $V(R^{\circ n})=n\,V(R)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. Base: $C(\mathbf 1)=0=0\cdot C(R)$, likewise $V$. Step: $C(R^{\circ(n+1)})
=C(R)+C(R^{\circ n})=C(R)+nC(R)=(n+1)C(R)$, and identically for $V$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Class invariance under batching]\label{cor:inv}
For $n\ge 1$, $R^{\circ n}$ lies in the same class as $R$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Multiplying $C(R)=V(R)$, $V(R)<C(R)$, or $C(R)<V(R)$ by the positive integer $n$ preserves the
relation, and $C(R^{\circ n})=nC(R)$, $V(R^{\circ n})=nV(R)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Batches and the Batch Quickness Theorem}

A menu is a finite list of recipes composed in sequence; write $\batch(L)$ for their
composition, so $C(\batch(L))=\sum_{R\in L}C(R)$ and $V(\batch(L))=\sum_{R\in L}V(R)$.

\begin{theorem}[Batch Quickness Theorem]\label{thm:batch}
Let $L$ be a menu of \emph{physical} recipes. Then $\batch(L)$ is quick if and only if every
recipe in $L$ is quick.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:pclose}, $\batch(L)$ is physical, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:spchar} it is
quick iff its speedup vanishes. By repeated application of Theorem~\ref{thm:spadd},
$\sodom(\batch(L))=\sum_{R\in L}\sodom(R)$. Each term is a non-negative integer (each $R$ is
physical), and a finite sum of non-negative integers is zero iff every summand is zero. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:spchar}, $\sodom(R)=0$ with $R$ physical is exactly quickness of $R$. Hence
$\batch(L)$ is quick iff every $R\in L$ is quick.
\end{proof}

In culinary terms, quickness of a physical menu is all-or-nothing: a single genuinely slow
dish forces the whole menu to be slow. In complexity terms, global slack vanishes exactly
when every local slack vanishes.

\section{The cooking ratio}

\begin{definition}[Cooking ratio]
For a recipe with $V(R)>0$, the \emph{cooking ratio} is $\rho(R)=C(R)/V(R)\in\mathbb Q_{\ge0}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Ratio characterization]\label{thm:ratio}
For a recipe with $V(R)>0$: $R$ is quick iff $\rho(R)=1$; traditional iff $\rho(R)>1$;
overhard iff $\rho(R)<1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Dividing the defining inequalities of each class by $V(R)>0$ preserves them.
\end{proof}

\section{Numerical experiments}

Classifying a corpus of sample recipes by their $(C,V)$ pairs:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccll}
\toprule
Recipe & $C$ & $V$ & class & $\rho=C/V$ \\
\midrule
Green salad    & 8    & 8  & quick       & 1.00 \\
Cheese plate   & 5    & 5  & quick       & 1.00 \\
Roast chicken  & 90   & 3  & traditional & 30.0 \\
Sourdough bread& 1440 & 2  & traditional & 720  \\
Beef stock     & 300  & 4  & traditional & 75.0 \\
Souffl\'e      & 40   & 60 & overhard    & 0.67 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Batching experiments confirm Theorem~\ref{thm:batch}: any menu of the two salads is quick,
while adding one traditional dish makes total cooking time strictly exceed total verification
time. Repetition experiments confirm Theorem~\ref{thm:scale}.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The theory is intentionally elementary; its interest lies in how faithfully a two-integer
model reproduces the qualitative architecture of complexity theory. Natural extensions
include: weighted/parallel cooking via a max-plus composition; a genuine reduction preorder
$R\preceq S$ with upward/downward-closed classes; cost hierarchies indexed by input size with
asymptotic separations $V=o(C)$; amortized speedup and averaging inequalities over menus; a
characterization of when composition preserves overhardness; and a probabilistic
(``taste a spoonful'') verification model.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a complete, elementary algebraic theory of recipes as resource-bounded
procedures, classifying every recipe as quick, traditional, or overhard, endowing recipes
with a commutative-monoid composition, and proving closure, additivity, scaling, and a Batch
Quickness Theorem that reduces global efficiency to local efficiency. Small as it is, the
theory captures the essential drama of $P$ versus $NP$: the relationship between the cost of
producing and the cost of verifying.

\end{document}

